window.LLM_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the shape of pred - target when pred is (4, 1) and target is (4,) ?","o":["(4,)","(4, 1)","(4, 4)","It raises a shape error"],"a":2,"w":"Shapes align from the right: (4, 1) and (1, 4) both stretch to (4, 4), with no error. The loss computed from it was 2.83 instead of 3.71."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In bf16, what is 1.0 + 0.001?","o":["1.0","1.001","1.0009765625","inf"],"a":0,"w":"With 7 mantissa bits, the gap just above 1.0 is 0.0078, so adding 0.001 rounds back to 1.0. fp16 gives 1.0009765625."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A timer around c = a @ b on CUDA tensors reads 0.05 ms, but with torch.cuda.synchronize() before stopping it reads 11.2 ms. Why?","o":["The first run was cached","The call only queued the work; the timer stopped before the GPU finished","synchronize() slows the GPU down","The CPU did the first multiply"],"a":1,"w":"CUDA operations are asynchronous. Without a synchronise (or CUDA events), you measure only the launch."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On this GPU, how much faster was a 4096 × 4096 matmul in bf16 than in plain fp32?","o":["About the same","About 1.4 times","About 2.8 times","About 130 times"],"a":2,"w":"88.8 against 31.6 TFLOPS. About 130 times is bf16 on the GPU against fp32 on the CPU; TF32 gave about 1.4 times."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why do training loops pin the memory of their input batches?","o":["Pinned memory uses fewer bytes","It stops batches being shuffled","The GPU cannot read pageable memory at all","Copies from pinned memory are faster and can overlap with compute"],"a":3,"w":"1 GiB copied in 18.6 ms from pinned memory (57.6 GB/s) against 48.7 ms from pageable memory (22 GB/s), and a non_blocking copy from pinned memory runs alongside compute."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why must a training loop call zero_grad() every step?","o":["It resets the learning rate","backward() adds to .grad , so old gradients would pile up","It frees the model's weights","It is only needed on the first step"],"a":1,"w":"Gradients accumulate by design. Without clearing them, each update uses the sum of all gradients so far; measured, the loss ended at 13,551 instead of 65."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The stability limit for plain gradient descent on the latency problem was 0.1015. What happened at a learning rate of 0.12?","o":["It converged faster than 0.1","It converged to a different answer","It stalled at the starting loss","The loss grew every step until it overflowed to infinity"],"a":3,"w":"Above the limit each step overshoots by more than the last: 6.8 million at step 10, then infinity."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An overfit-one-batch test gets stuck with the loss at exactly the variance of the targets. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The learning rate is too small","The model has too many parameters","The loss compares predictions with the wrong targets, for example through a broadcasting bug","The GPU ran out of memory"],"a":2,"w":"When every prediction is compared with every target, the best possible answer is the mean, and the loss is then the targets' variance: 0.6424 here."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did the forward pass with gradients use 704 MiB more memory but only 128 MiB without them?","o":["Autograd keeps intermediate activations for the backward pass, plus the weight gradients","Gradients are always stored in fp64","torch.no_grad() compresses the weights","The model is copied for the backward pass"],"a":0,"w":"Seven saved activations of 64 MiB, a 128 MiB fp32 copy of the output for the loss, and 128 MiB of weight gradients. Without gradients, each activation can be freed as soon as the next layer has used it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does the cost of reverse-mode autograd compare with finite differences for a model with N parameters?","o":["Both need about N forward passes","Autograd needs about two forward passes' worth for all N; finite differences need about 2N forward passes","Finite differences are cheaper but less accurate","Autograd needs one backward pass per parameter"],"a":1,"w":"One backward pass yields every gradient at once, which is what makes training models with billions of parameters possible."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The naive softmax exp(z) / exp(z).sum() returned NaN on GPT-2's next-token logits even in fp32. Why?","o":["The largest logit overflowed to infinity","Every logit was around −124 or lower, so every exp() underflowed to 0 and the result was 0 / 0","The vocabulary is too large for fp32","GPT-2's logits contain NaN"],"a":1,"w":"exp(−124) ≈ 10⁻⁵⁴ is far below fp32's smallest value. Subtracting the max (−124.0) puts the largest term at exp(0) = 1."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Above roughly what value does exp(x) overflow to infinity in fp16?","o":["2","88.7","11.1","65,504"],"a":2,"w":"fp16's largest value is 65,504, and ln(65,504) ≈ 11.09. In fp32 and bf16 the limit is about 88.7."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"GPT-2 gave the correct next token a probability of 0.1592. What is that token's cross-entropy loss?","o":["About 1.84","About 0.16","About 0.84","About 6.28"],"a":0,"w":"−ln(0.1592) ≈ 1.84 nats, the value measured for \" 200\"."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A new language model with a 50,257-token vocabulary should have a loss close to what at step zero?","o":["0","1","3.9","10.8, which is ln(50,257)"],"a":3,"w":"Near-uniform predictions score ln(V) on every token; a freshly initialised head measured 11.02."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does RMSNorm differ from LayerNorm?","o":["It normalises across the batch instead of across channels","It divides by the root mean square without subtracting the mean, and has no bias","It has no learned parameters at all","It is applied only to the output layer"],"a":1,"w":"Both act on each token's vector. RMSNorm keeps only the rescaling and a learned gain; on GPT-2's vector the mean was already near zero (0.03)."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A model's mean negative log-likelihood on a text is 3.09 nats per token. What is its perplexity?","o":["3.09","About 9.5","About 22","About 1,230"],"a":2,"w":"e^3.09 ≈ 22, GPT-2's perplexity on the JSON body."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is a sequence's probability kept as a sum of log-probabilities?","o":["The product of many probabilities underflows to zero","Logs make the model more accurate","GPUs cannot multiply probabilities","It removes the need for softmax"],"a":0,"w":"For a 296-token passage the product was exactly zero after 33 tokens in fp32 and 210 in fp64; the log-likelihood was an ordinary −1,055.2."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the entropy of a uniform distribution over GPT-2's 50,257 tokens?","o":["1 bit","10.8 bits","50,257 bits","About 15.6 bits"],"a":3,"w":"log₂ 50,257 ≈ 15.6 bits. 10.8 is the same quantity in nats (ln 50,257)."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After 10 samples, \" 200\" appeared once (10%), though its probability is 15.9%. What does that tell you?","o":["The sampler is biased","Nothing much: 10 draws are far too few, and 10,000 gave 16.1%","The model's probabilities are wrong","Sampling should use the top token only"],"a":1,"w":"A frequency from n draws has error of order 1/√n; with 10 draws, three of the six likeliest tokens never appeared at all."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can a model score a whole training sequence in one forward pass but must generate one token at a time?","o":["Training uses a different model","Generation needs more memory","When scoring, every earlier token is already known; when generating, each next token depends on the one just chosen","Scoring only looks at the last token"],"a":2,"w":"A causal model predicts every position from its prefix in one pass; the results matched step-by-step scoring to within 5.4 × 10⁻⁵."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many bytes does the euro sign € (U+20AC) take in UTF-8?","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":2,"w":"E2 82 AC: a 3-byte sequence, as for Devanagari, Japanese and Chinese characters."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"For the string \"😀\", what do Python's len() and JavaScript's .length return?","o":["1 and 1","1 and 2","4 and 4","2 and 4"],"a":1,"w":"Python counts code points (1); JavaScript counts UTF-16 units, and characters above U+FFFF need two (a surrogate pair)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"\"café\" typed as é (U+00E9) and as e + U+0301 compare unequal. What makes them equal?","o":["Lower-casing both","Encoding both as UTF-16","Stripping whitespace","Normalising both to NFC"],"a":3,"w":"NFC composes e + combining accent into U+00E9; GPT-2 had tokenised them as 3 and 4 tokens."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did a Python function indented with spaces cost GPT-2 29 tokens but Qwen2.5 only 18?","o":["GPT-2's tokeniser has no tokens for runs of spaces, so each space is a token","GPT-2 cannot read Python","Qwen2.5 removes indentation","Spaces are 2 bytes in UTF-8"],"a":0,"w":"32 spaces were 32 GPT-2 tokens but 1 Qwen2.5 token; with tabs the same function was 20 and 16."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A zero-width space is inserted inside the word \"ignore\" in a user's message. What is the main risk?","o":["The model can no longer read the word","UTF-8 decoding fails","A filter matching the string \"ignore\" misses it, while the model still reads it","The message becomes longer than the context window"],"a":2,"w":"The word still looks the same and a model can read it, but it is now a different string, here 4 GPT-2 tokens instead of 1. Strip or flag format characters before filtering."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many FLOPs does multiplying an (m × k) matrix by a (k × n) matrix take?","o":["m · k · n","2 · m · k · n","m · n","2 · (m · k + k · n)"],"a":1,"w":"Each of the m · n outputs is a length-k dot product: k multiplies and k additions."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"For GPT-2 on 1,024 tokens, the rule 2 · N · T gave 254.9 GFLOPs but PyTorch counted 291.6. What did the rule leave out?","o":["The language-model head","The MLP layers","Attention's score and value multiplies, which grow with T²","The backward pass"],"a":2,"w":"Attention cost 38.7 GFLOPs at this length, and its share keeps growing with sequence length."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"This GPU reads memory at about 830 GB/s and does 96.6 TFLOPS of bf16 compute. What does its ridge point of about 116 FLOPs per byte mean?","o":["Work doing fewer FLOPs per byte moved is limited by memory bandwidth, not arithmetic","It can do at most 116 FLOPs per second","Matrices larger than 116 rows are slow","bf16 values take 116 bytes"],"a":0,"w":"Below the ridge, the arithmetic units wait for data; only reading fewer bytes, or reusing each byte for more work, helps."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"One row multiplied by a 4096 × 4096 bf16 weight ran at 0.43 TFLOPS. Why so slow?","o":["The GPU was not warmed up","bf16 is slow for single rows","The multiply was done on the CPU","It reads all 32 MiB of weights to do only 2 FLOPs per weight: memory-bound"],"a":3,"w":"Its 0.078 ms was spent almost entirely reading the weights. With 16,384 rows the same weights did 96.6 TFLOPS."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does cosine similarity remove compared with a raw dot product?","o":["The sign","The effect of the vectors' lengths, keeping only their directions","The need for a matrix multiply","Small values"],"a":1,"w":"It is the dot product divided by both lengths, so it ranges from −1 to 1 whatever the vectors' sizes."}];
